WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:02.700
Instructor: Okay, so next up I wanna show another example

00:02.700 --> 00:06.150
of how reflogs can help save lost commits

00:06.150 --> 00:08.340
or seemingly lost commits.

00:08.340 --> 00:11.250
This time I'm going to perform a rebase

00:11.250 --> 00:14.010
which when I first taught it, I made it very clear

00:14.010 --> 00:16.620
that rebasing rewrites commits.

00:16.620 --> 00:20.250
It makes new commits and those old ones are gone,

00:20.250 --> 00:24.630
at least they seem to be gone until we talked about reflogs.

00:24.630 --> 00:28.530
So let me show you an example. Let's make a branch here.

00:28.530 --> 00:33.530
I'm working in the same vegetables, seeds, little demo.

00:33.570 --> 00:36.870
Let's do a switch. How about flowers?

00:36.870 --> 00:39.180
So we'll pretend I'm working on a feature branch,

00:39.180 --> 00:43.050
the flowers feature branch where I will be, I don't know,

00:43.050 --> 00:43.920
this is veggies.

00:43.920 --> 00:47.760
I'll be working on the different flowers that I wanna plant

00:47.760 --> 00:50.910
or that I'm starting in my garage or something like that.

00:50.910 --> 00:52.920
So what should I put in here?

00:52.920 --> 00:56.850
Why don't we just make our first commit, git add flowers.

00:56.850 --> 01:00.513
We'll do a git commit dash m, add flowers file.

01:01.740 --> 01:03.330
So I'm pretending, this is a feature

01:03.330 --> 01:04.980
so I'll have a couple of commits.

01:05.820 --> 01:10.820
Let's see, I'll add some flowers about some salmon zinnias.

01:11.160 --> 01:16.160
And what are some other, queen lime zinnias.

01:17.820 --> 01:22.290
Okay, so I'll do a git commit dash am just to speed it up,

01:22.290 --> 01:27.290
add some zinnias, and then on the next line,

01:28.440 --> 01:30.750
what's another type of flower that I grow?

01:30.750 --> 01:32.853
How about some dahlias?

01:33.720 --> 01:38.433
So we've got old rose dahlia.

01:40.710 --> 01:44.250
And there's another. I'll just, dinnerplate dahlias.

01:44.250 --> 01:47.400
I don't even know if that's really a specific one.

01:47.400 --> 01:49.890
Okay so I'll do a git commit dash am.

01:49.890 --> 01:52.590
I'm just gonna add one more commit after this one.

01:52.590 --> 01:56.733
Add dahlias to flowers list.

01:57.630 --> 01:59.250
And then how about some amaranth,

01:59.250 --> 02:04.250
so coral fountain amaranth, and also hot biscuits amaranth.

02:11.280 --> 02:16.280
Git commit dash am, add amaranth to flowers.

02:19.170 --> 02:21.690
Okay, I lied. I'll do one last one.

02:21.690 --> 02:23.700
I'll add some celosia.

02:23.700 --> 02:26.250
Some really cool flowers by the way, these celosias.

02:26.250 --> 02:29.070
Coral reef celosia, I'll just leave it at that.

02:29.070 --> 02:32.610
Git commit dash am, add celosia.

02:32.610 --> 02:36.540
Okay, so I'm done with my feature. This is my feature.

02:36.540 --> 02:39.420
It's not exciting, but I have what,

02:39.420 --> 02:42.270
four commits or so on this flowers branch.

02:42.270 --> 02:44.760
I want to eventually merge them into master

02:44.760 --> 02:48.390
but before I do that, I'm going to rebase.

02:48.390 --> 02:51.030
I'm gonna do an interactive rebase to get rid

02:51.030 --> 02:53.040
of some of these commits

02:53.040 --> 02:56.100
or combine them or just, you know, clean up.

02:56.100 --> 02:58.530
If you did not watch the section on rebasing

02:58.530 --> 03:01.653
and specifically the portion about interactive rebasing,

03:03.090 --> 03:05.940
I mean I can't imagine it would make any sense.

03:05.940 --> 03:07.920
So I would check that out.

03:07.920 --> 03:09.630
The point is more so going to be

03:09.630 --> 03:11.460
about fixing it after the fact.

03:11.460 --> 03:15.390
So I'm gonna do a git rebase back to here,

03:15.390 --> 03:16.340
so an interactive rebase.

03:16.340 --> 03:20.430
So this is head one, two, three, four.

03:20.430 --> 03:22.170
So I'll do head tilde four,

03:22.170 --> 03:26.553
git rebase dash I head tilde four.

03:28.320 --> 03:29.430
And there we are.

03:29.430 --> 03:31.890
You can see it opens up this file

03:31.890 --> 03:34.200
for me to do my interactive rebasing in.

03:34.200 --> 03:36.900
But let's say that I want to, I don't know,

03:36.900 --> 03:40.863
we'll fix up these two or maybe these three.

03:41.820 --> 03:44.490
So that will, if you don't remember,

03:44.490 --> 03:46.110
you can see down here, fix up.

03:46.110 --> 03:49.050
We'll squash them, basically mush 'em

03:49.050 --> 03:52.110
into the next previous commit

03:52.110 --> 03:56.130
but it's going to discard the log message as well.

03:56.130 --> 04:01.130
So if I replace pick with instead F or fix up,

04:01.740 --> 04:04.620
and I can do this for multiple, fix up,

04:04.620 --> 04:06.120
can do it for all of them.

04:06.120 --> 04:10.380
I'll do these three. So fix up, fix up, fix up.

04:10.380 --> 04:12.180
What you'll see is that the contents,

04:12.180 --> 04:14.550
the actual changes in the commits don't go away, right?

04:14.550 --> 04:18.840
We don't lose that work but we will be rewriting history.

04:18.840 --> 04:20.280
So I am squashing them.

04:20.280 --> 04:23.730
I'm melding all of these down into this commit.

04:23.730 --> 04:27.030
And so I'm gonna save and close this window

04:27.030 --> 04:29.490
and it tells us, successfully rebased and updated.

04:29.490 --> 04:30.963
Let's look at our git log.

04:32.310 --> 04:35.340
And now we have this one commit here. It has a new hash.

04:35.340 --> 04:37.260
We rewrote history

04:37.260 --> 04:39.990
and I could repeat the process one more time as well.

04:39.990 --> 04:43.053
I won't go back that far, but how about head tilde two?

04:44.160 --> 04:48.060
And maybe I decide I want to fix up this one,

04:48.060 --> 04:50.520
I wanna mush it back into this one

04:50.520 --> 04:52.890
and then instead of doing a pick here,

04:52.890 --> 04:56.070
maybe I want to reword the commit message instead

04:56.070 --> 04:58.020
of add flowers file.

04:58.020 --> 04:59.460
So I can do that.

04:59.460 --> 05:01.890
I'll rename this or not rename it, but come up

05:01.890 --> 05:04.740
with a new commit message and then mush this one into it.

05:05.940 --> 05:08.730
And now it opens up, when I close outta that window,

05:08.730 --> 05:11.910
it opens up the commit message that I can edit.

05:11.910 --> 05:14.640
So instead of saying add flowers file,

05:14.640 --> 05:19.530
let's go with add flowers, you know,

05:19.530 --> 05:24.000
flowers feature or how about just add flowers,

05:24.000 --> 05:27.720
add list of seeds, okay?

05:27.720 --> 05:30.510
So I'm basically taking all that work I did

05:30.510 --> 05:33.660
and combining it into one new commit with a new message.

05:33.660 --> 05:35.460
We talked about this with interactive rebasing.

05:35.460 --> 05:36.900
We can clean up our history.

05:36.900 --> 05:39.270
So if this is my feature, it's simple

05:39.270 --> 05:41.790
but this is the commit.

05:41.790 --> 05:42.960
I'll X outta there.

05:42.960 --> 05:45.180
And now if I do a git log,

05:45.180 --> 05:48.300
we just have this one commit hash right there

05:48.300 --> 05:51.570
and this contains everything for the flowers branch.

05:51.570 --> 05:55.260
So if I decided, that was a big mistake,

05:55.260 --> 05:57.090
or for whatever reason I want

05:57.090 --> 05:59.520
to access those individual commits,

05:59.520 --> 06:02.220
I even want the commit messages or I don't know,

06:02.220 --> 06:06.333
I want something from the past, we don't have to fear.

06:07.260 --> 06:09.423
We can use reflogs. Reflogs are here.

06:10.290 --> 06:12.540
So git reflog show

06:12.540 --> 06:16.980
and then we can do it for flowers and take a look.

06:16.980 --> 06:20.160
Every single one of those commits is still here,

06:20.160 --> 06:21.540
every commit hash.

06:21.540 --> 06:23.400
So this is when I added the flowers file.

06:23.400 --> 06:25.640
Here's where I did some zinnias, some dahlias,

06:25.640 --> 06:28.680
amarants, celosia, they're all here.

06:28.680 --> 06:29.790
So I have to decide,

06:29.790 --> 06:32.160
you know, what point I wanna go back to.

06:32.160 --> 06:35.100
But if I want to go to this point, I want all

06:35.100 --> 06:38.670
of those prior commits, all five commits that are gone now.

06:38.670 --> 06:42.480
I can copy this commit hash or I could copy this,

06:42.480 --> 06:44.490
but I'll copy the commit hash.

06:44.490 --> 06:45.870
The commit is still stored

06:45.870 --> 06:49.230
in the dot git directory and the objects directory.

06:49.230 --> 06:50.910
Now it won't be there forever,

06:50.910 --> 06:52.290
but it's definitely there now.

06:52.290 --> 06:54.300
Git goes through periodically as we talked about

06:54.300 --> 06:56.160
and it cleans up some of these,

06:56.160 --> 06:58.170
it does this thing called garbage collection

06:58.170 --> 07:01.560
if it notices git objects that are not reachable.

07:01.560 --> 07:05.040
So for example, this is not reachable from another commit.

07:05.040 --> 07:06.630
That is part of our history.

07:06.630 --> 07:10.500
It's stored in there and so we can basically say, git,

07:10.500 --> 07:14.880
I want you now to point your branch to this commit instead.

07:14.880 --> 07:15.750
And this commit was

07:15.750 --> 07:18.450
on its own, totally alone, not connected.

07:18.450 --> 07:21.630
And now after I've reset back to this commit,

07:21.630 --> 07:23.970
it will be hooked back into the grid.

07:23.970 --> 07:27.360
We'll have a pointer for our branch referencing to this.

07:27.360 --> 07:30.450
All right, so this is what I want. I can copy this.

07:30.450 --> 07:32.850
Just remember, these commits are not part

07:32.850 --> 07:34.170
of my history anymore, right?

07:34.170 --> 07:38.670
I don't see any of those commits. Just do one line.

07:38.670 --> 07:39.990
None of them are here.

07:39.990 --> 07:40.823
The hashes aren't here,

07:40.823 --> 07:43.680
the messages aren't here because I fixed them up.

07:43.680 --> 07:46.650
I merged 'em all together and gave them a new message too.

07:46.650 --> 07:49.470
I boiled it down to this one commit.

07:49.470 --> 07:52.620
So if I want to reset back to that, I can do that.

07:52.620 --> 07:56.640
Git reset, dash dash hard, not heard,

07:56.640 --> 07:58.530
and then pass in that commit hash.

07:58.530 --> 08:00.600
And this says the flower branch,

08:00.600 --> 08:03.060
that reference, should now point to this commit

08:03.060 --> 08:06.900
that we found in the garbage, we found in the reflog.

08:06.900 --> 08:09.810
And it says head is now at add celosia.

08:09.810 --> 08:13.470
Well, let's look at git log. Hey, look at that.

08:13.470 --> 08:15.840
We have all of those commits.

08:15.840 --> 08:17.460
And the reason this works is

08:17.460 --> 08:21.150
that every commit has, inside of it in that file,

08:21.150 --> 08:24.300
it also stores its parents or parents.

08:24.300 --> 08:27.330
So if we reset based on this one commit,

08:27.330 --> 08:30.603
we also get the parent commit and its parent and so on.

08:31.560 --> 08:35.460
So this ends up doing exactly what I was trying to show you.

08:35.460 --> 08:37.680
We've rebased, we rewrote history,

08:37.680 --> 08:39.300
we eliminated these commits,

08:39.300 --> 08:42.930
we combined them, we renamed them or redid their messages,

08:42.930 --> 08:44.340
we smooshed 'em together.

08:44.340 --> 08:47.070
They were lost or apparently lost,

08:47.070 --> 08:49.200
but I was able to rescue it.

08:49.200 --> 08:51.360
For whatever reason, I decided I wanted to.

08:51.360 --> 08:53.730
I'm able to go back and undo that.

08:53.730 --> 08:57.030
That rebase, it's basically like it never happened.

08:57.030 --> 09:00.390
But actually in our reflog,

09:00.390 --> 09:03.750
we can see that for whatever reason I wanted

09:03.750 --> 09:08.310
to go back and undo the undoing, I could.

09:08.310 --> 09:11.100
I could go to this commit hash right here.

09:11.100 --> 09:13.860
This does not exist right now in my history

09:13.860 --> 09:15.570
but it's in the reflog.

09:15.570 --> 09:17.790
So that object is still stored in git,

09:17.790 --> 09:20.610
but we don't see AB nine when I do git log.

09:20.610 --> 09:23.730
But I could reset to that, but I won't, all right?

09:23.730 --> 09:25.230
So I hope now you can see some

09:25.230 --> 09:27.270
of the utility in the reflog.

09:27.270 --> 09:30.300
Remember, it's local and it's not permanent.

09:30.300 --> 09:32.640
But in a lot of situations, especially

09:32.640 --> 09:34.800
if you do something that seems permanent

09:34.800 --> 09:39.800
or seems bad, rebasing or resetting, a hard reset,

09:40.860 --> 09:42.720
those still can be undone

09:42.720 --> 09:44.620
if you know how to look in the reflog.
